        "description": "Conjecture: For a natural infinite family of formal theorem environments {E_n} with theorem T_n and finite dependency DAGs of reusable lemmas, there exists a sharp threshold p_c(n) for random axiom/lemma retention such that if each nonlogical premise is independently retained with probability p > (1+eps)p_c(n), then T_n remains provable with high probability by a proof of length polylogarithmically inflated from optimum, while for p < (1-eps)p_c(n), every proof either fails to exist or requires superpolynomial blowup with high probability. Test: Construct theorem families with explicit dependency graphs from formal libraries, randomly subsample available premises, and measure provability and shortest-proof inflation versus p; confirmation is a narrow transition window and finite-size scaling law, refutation is a broad/no threshold or no consistent scaling across families. Impact: Would introduce a statistical-mechanics theory of proof robustness, quantify redundancy in mathematical libraries, and guide minimal-core extraction, curriculum design for theorem provers, and resilient automated reasoning under incomplete knowledge.",
