        "description": "Conjecture: For a natural family of theorem-proving tasks with formally checkable ground truth, if one builds the proof-state graph explored by a neural prover and equips it with an embedding-induced simplicial complex, then there exists a universal critical defect-density parameter \\(\\delta_c\\) such that whenever the density of nonfillable 1-cycles per explored node exceeds \\(\\delta_c\\), the asymptotic rate of invalid-but-high-confidence proof proposals jumps discontinuously; below \\(\\delta_c\\), this rate stays sublinear in search budget. Test: Run controlled experiments across theorem families, model sizes, and search strategies; compute defect density from the explored proof graph and measure the fraction of syntactically plausible but verifier-rejected proof attempts. The conjecture is supported if a consistent phase transition appears at approximately task-invariant critical density, and refuted if no sharp threshold or no cross-family universality is observed. Impact: This would give a mathematically grounded early-warning invariant for neural proof hallucination, suggest topology-aware search regularizers, and connect homological phase transitions to reliability in automated reasoning.",
