        "title": "Proof Geodesics in Theorem Spaces via Optimal Transport",
        "description": "Conjecture: For a natural parametric family of formal theorem statements {T_n} with machine-checkable proofs, there exists a canonical embedding of proofs into a metric measure space such that minimal proofs of T_n and T_{n+1} are connected by low-Wasserstein-cost geodesics, and phase transitions in this transport cost predict when new lemmas become necessary. Test: Choose a concrete family (e.g. Ramsey bounds, graph extremal statements, or finite-group identities), enumerate or synthesize many proofs, embed them by dependency/term structure, compute OT distances between proof distributions for adjacent n, and check whether sharp jumps in transport cost coincide with empirically observed changes in reusable lemma bases or proof length scaling. Refutation occurs if no stable embedding yields predictive transport signals beyond trivial size effects. Impact: This would create a quantitative theory of how mathematics deforms across parameter families, enabling automated transfer of lemmas, curriculum design for theorem provers, and early detection of conceptual bottlenecks in formal research.",
