        "id": "fd_0149",
        "title": "Arithmetic Resonance Windows in Gradient Flow on Modular Tasks",
        "description": "Conjecture: For a natural family of neural networks with integer or rational initial weights trained by gradient descent on modular arithmetic tasks over F_p (for varying primes p), there exists a nontrivial infinite set of primes P* such that after lifting training dynamics to p-adic coordinates, the valuation growth rates of specific weight-combination invariants undergo sharp resonance transitions exactly when the task's hidden algebraic symmetry group acquires extra low-dimensional representations over F_p. These resonance primes predict both accelerated grokking and abrupt drops in proof-extractable circuit complexity of the learned function. Test: Train the same architecture across many primes p on tasks like modular inversion, discrete log on bounded domains, and polynomial identity recognition; compute p-adic valuations of tracked invariant polynomials in the weights during training; independently compute representation-theoretic data of the task symmetry over F_p; confirm/refute by checking whether statistically significant phase transitions in learning time and extracted circuit size align with the predicted representation-change primes more often than chance and disappear on control tasks without such symmetry changes. Impact: Would create a new bridge between modular representation theory, p-adic dynamics, and mechanistic interpretability, potentially enabling arithmetic prediction of when neural systems suddenly become algorithmic and when compact formal proofs/circuits can be extracted from trained models.",
