        "description": "Conjecture: For transformer models trained on families of formally checkable mathematical tasks with controllable proof depth, there exists an architecture-independent integer invariant r(n), defined from the rank growth of layerwise token-to-token Jacobian phase portraits, such that the median minimal proof depth solved at task size n is sharply thresholded by r(n): below a critical phase-rank the success probability stays near 0, and above it stays near 1, uniformly across model scales after rescaling by width. Test: Generate theorem families with tunable proof depth (e.g. bounded-resolution SAT, finite group identities, graph extremal lemmas), train transformers of varying width/depth, compute the proposed phase-rank statistic from internal activations, and check whether success curves collapse onto a universal threshold law; refuted if no architecture-stable threshold or predictive collapse appears. Impact: Would provide a measurable internal order parameter for emergent reasoning, enabling principled scaling laws, mechanistic interpretability of proof discovery, and architecture design targeted at formal mathematical competence.",
