        "description": "Conjecture: There exists an explicit infinite family of arithmetic graphs G_n (for example, graphs on {1,...,n} with edges defined by a modular quadratic residue relation) such that for their clique/flag complexes X_n, the first index k where homology stabilizes under barycentric subdivision depends nontrivially on the coefficient field: for infinitely many n and distinct primes p,q, H_i(sd^k X_n; F_p) becomes subdivision-stable strictly earlier than H_i(sd^k X_n; F_q) for some fixed i. Test: Construct candidate graph families, compute flag complexes and persistent homology of repeated subdivisions over multiple finite fields, and verify whether the stabilization threshold k_p(n) differs across primes for infinitely many instances; refutation occurs if thresholds empirically coincide across all tested primes and the family admits a prime-independent proof. Impact: This would reveal a new arithmetic layer in combinatorial topology, linking graph-defined complexes, subdivision dynamics, and coefficient-sensitive topological phase transitions, with implications for arithmetic TDA and finite-field-sensitive algorithms.",
