        "description": "Conjecture: There exists a recursively enumerable family C of finitely presented, residually finite groups such that for any G, H in C with presentation length at most n, if the counts of epimorphisms |Epi(G,Q)| and |Epi(H,Q)| agree for every finite group Q of order at most poly(n), then G and H are isomorphic. Test: Generate large corpora of finitely presented residually finite groups, compute low-order finite quotients and epimorphism count vectors, and search for collisions among non-isomorphic groups; confirmation is absence of collisions up to growing n together with a proof for a natural subclass (e.g. right-angled Artin groups, surface groups, or small-cancellation families), while any explicit non-isomorphic pair in C with identical low-order quotient statistics refutes it. Impact: This would turn finite-quotient data into a practical complete invariant on structured classes, linking profinite rigidity, computational group theory, and automated theorem discovery, and could yield new algorithms for group isomorphism via bounded finite-shadow fingerprints.",
