        "description": "Conjecture: For any sufficiently large family of proof-state transition graphs generated from a fixed formal domain under increasing resource bounds R, there exists a coarse-graining operator T on proof-state graphs (contracting strongly connected micro-regions and degree-2 transient chains) such that the normalized persistent homology profile of T^k(G_R) converges, as k increases and then R -> infinity, to a domain-specific fixed point. Moreover, domains with polynomial-time proof search and domains with inherently exponential proof search yield distinct fixed-point universality classes detectable by Betti decay exponents and spectral dimension. Test: Construct proof-state graphs for multiple formal domains and resource levels, repeatedly apply the coarse-graining operator, compute persistence diagrams / Betti curves / spectral dimension at each scale, and check whether (i) trajectories converge to stable invariants within a domain and (ii) the limiting invariants separate easy-vs-hard proof regimes. Refutation occurs if no stable scale-invariant profile emerges or if fixed points fail to distinguish complexity classes. Impact: This would introduce a renormalization-group style theory of theorem proving, enabling scale-independent hardness diagnostics, transfer of complexity predictions across formal systems, and new architecture priors for proof-search algorithms.",
