        "id": "fd_0379",
        "title": "Arithmetic Resonance Predicts Prime-Sensitive Torsion in Persistent Homology",
        "description": "Conjecture: For any finite filtered CW complex X with integer boundary matrices, define the p-adic persistence profile B_X(p) to be the multiset of barcode intervals weighted by the p-adic valuations of the corresponding Smith normal form torsion invariants. Then there exists a finite-dimensional 'resonance signature' extracted solely from the valuations of minors of the boundary matrices such that, outside a finite exceptional set of primes, B_X(p) is determined by this signature and varies quasi-polynomially with p on congruence classes. Test: Compute Smith normal forms and minor-valuation data for broad families of filtered complexes (random flag complexes, arithmetic simplicial complexes, knot filtrations, configuration-space filtrations) and check whether primes in the same predicted congruence class yield identical torsion barcode structure outside finitely many exceptions; refute by finding an infinite family where identical resonance signatures produce asymptotically different B_X(p). Impact: This would create a new bridge between arithmetic geometry, p-adic analysis, and topological data analysis, enabling prime-aware compression of torsion persistence and revealing hidden number-theoretic structure in topological signals.",
